><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251112095703+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="d79f8a91-86e5-4a6d-969b-ed3c6109042b" date="D:20251112095703+10'00'" name="8a26f571-a88f-4511-8453-f246bfc31571" icon="Comment" page="6" rect="322.495239,246.002197,346.495239,270.002197" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>These results suggest that coaches and support staff may&#xD;need to find alternative strategies for increasing adolescent&#xD;athletes’ sleep opportunities to .8 hours per night on training&#xD;camps, particularly if the athletes already exhibit satisfactory&#xD;sleep quality (as may be assessed with the PSQI).&#xD;Examples of alternative strategies may include using different&#xD;sleep hygiene strategies and scheduling meetings and training&#xD;sessions to provide longer sleep durations.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="6" rect="612.000000,178.002197,796.000000,270.002197"
/></text
